\name{circplot}
\alias{circplot}
\alias{clockplot}
\title{
Circular plot and clock plot
}

\description{
Circular plot of circular data, with the data stacked around the unit circle
and an arrow pointing to the mean direction. \code{clockplot} draws the same
plot as a 24-hour clock face, for data that are times of the day.
}

\usage{
circplot(u, mu = NULL, rads = TRUE, labels = "both", type = "auto",
bins = 72, maxh = 0.6, col = "#E76F51", lwd = 4, pt.col = "#2A9D8F", cex = 1.3,
disc.col = "#F4F1EA", border.col = "#555555")

clockplot(x, mu = NULL, rads = TRUE, type = "auto", bins = 48, maxh = 0.6,
col = "#E76F51", lwd = 4, pt.col = "#2A9D8F", cex = 1.3,
disc.col = "#F4F1EA", border.col = "#555555")
}

\arguments{
\item{u}{
A vector with the circular data expressed in radians, or degrees.
}
\item{x}{
A vector with times of the day expressed as angles, in radians or degrees.
The angle 0 corresponds to 24:00 and a full turn (\eqn{2\pi} or 360 degrees)
corresponds to 24 hours, so \eqn{\pi/2} is 06:00 and \eqn{\pi} is 12:00.
}
\item{mu}{
The estimated circular mean or a circular location estimate, in the same units
as \code{u} (or \code{x}). If this is not available the circular mean is
calculated internally.
}
\item{rads}{
If the data (and \code{mu}) are expressed in degrees set this to FALSE.
}
\item{labels}{
Which angle labels to show inside the disc: "both" (degrees above radians),
"degrees" or "radians". Only used by \code{circplot}.
}
\item{type}{
How to draw the data: "points" stacks the observations as points, "bars" draws
a circular histogram with one bar per bin, and "auto" uses points when no bin
contains more than 15 observations and bars otherwise.
}
\item{bins}{
The number of bins (equal arcs) the circle is split into. For \code{clockplot}
the default of 48 bins corresponds to half-hour intervals.
}
\item{maxh}{
The maximum radial extent of the stacked points or bars outside the circle.
}
\item{col}{
The colour of the arrow pointing to the mean direction.
}
\item{lwd}{
The thickness of the above arrow of the circular mean.
}
\item{pt.col}{
The fill colour of the points or bars.
}
\item{cex}{
The size of the points. It is reduced automatically when the stacks would
exceed \code{maxh}.
}
\item{disc.col}{
The colour inside the disc.
}
\item{border.col}{
The colour of the circle.
}
}

\details{
The observations are grouped into \code{bins} equal arcs. With \code{type = "points"}
they are stacked outwards from the unit circle; with \code{type = "bars"} each arc
gets a bar whose length is proportional to its count, which is preferable for
large samples. The mean direction is computed as
\eqn{\omega = atan2(\bar{S}, \bar{C}) \bmod 2\pi}, where
\eqn{\bar{C} = n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^n\cos\theta_i} and
\eqn{\bar{S} = n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^n\sin\theta_i}.

\code{clockplot} places 24:00 at the top and runs clockwise, as on a clock, with
06:00 on the right, 12:00 at the bottom and 18:00 on the left. Labels are shown
every 3 hours and tick marks every hour.
}

\value{
\code{circplot}: a circular plot. The mean direction (in radians) is returned invisibly.

\code{clockplot}: a clock plot. A list is returned invisibly with components:
\item{mu}{The mean direction in radians.}
\item{time}{The mean time of the day as a character string "HH:MM".}
}

